Connecting through SSH

You will need the OpenSSH-client on your local machine and the OpenSSH-server on the vps (be default it should have it)

You can connect through username and password (and you will on your first connection) or by a pair of keys.

When generating a pair of keys (on your local machine) you will have a public key and a private key. The public key will be shared with your vps (paste it on ~/.ssh/authorized-keys), so that it knows your pc can connect.
The private key won't be shared, instead it will be used to generate a signing that, when shared with the server that you are trying to connect to, can prove you have the private key by verifying the signing with the public key.

How to:

1. you need to make sure there is a /.ssh directory.
mkdir ~/.ssh

2. then you will generate the pair of keys with a command like this:
ssh-keygen -t ed25519 -a 100

(rsa is also considered reosonable, but I heard this is the best way security wise)
it will ask for a passphrase for the directory, is it recommended you use one but not required

3. You will find two files inside ~/.ssh/
ls ~/.ssh
id_ed25519 and id_ed25519.pub

the first is the private key (not shared) and the second is the public key (which will share with the vps host)

4. Share the public key
inside the vps host (first time connect through ssh {USER}@{VPS_ID} with the values that provider gives you), copy the result of

cat ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub (on your local machine)
inside
~/.ssh/authorized_keys (on the vps)

You can copy the key to your user's ssh folder or your root's ssh folder, that will allow you to ssh through a pair of keys with root or normal user

5. Try to connect to your vps through ssh, if it asks for the user's password (different for the passphrase) then something went wrong, otherwise you successfully set up a pair of keys to connect to your vps.
